A||||35||SCCA FIERO||Orge, help.....Problem with my truck||||
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||08-27-99||08:37 PM||||I know this is the Fiero Forum but if I don't get my truck fixed soon I will have to drive my Fiero in the snow that will be here soon.<p>My 94 S10 with the enhanced 4.3 has 85,000 miles and here is a bit of history. About a year ago my heater core got a hole in it. I replaced that. I was noticing coolant loss too, but not on the ground. Then I was pulling my tool trailer up a canyon and it all the sudden lost power. My trailer isn't very heavy and the power loss is more noticeable the harder I push the GO pedal. In January my father-in-law replaced the head gaskets. He said something ate away the passenger bank gasket. He replaced both. It ran great! Then it started again. I told him and he said it may have a cracked block. He put something in it to try to stop the leak and about that time it would spit black shit out of the tailpipe on start up. It also runs very rough and still loses power the harder I push the throttle. When it idles and I watch the tailpipe and give it throttle it shoots dark smoke. OH, I did a pressure test and it held 15lbs. for half an hour.<p>P.S. I just had emissions done on it. It has to be less than 220 ppm on carbon and Hydro. It was 2000!!! at idle and at 2500 rpm. I think that is as high the machine reads. Of course I failed. This truck came from California and I think it has California emissions(whatever that is)<p>Is it a sensor? How hard is it to locate a crack in a block? I am going to start ripping things off of it til I find a problem starting with the heads. Is that a good idea? What would you do? (Anyone with an idea is welcome to post) Thanks for any help you may give.
Z||000001||Patrick||08-27-99||09:49 PM||||<br>Well, first of all, I'd get a good set of tire chains for the Fiero....<p><p><IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p><p><p>
Z||000002||batboy||08-27-99||10:12 PM||||First thing to do is a compression check of all cylinders.
Z||000003||theogre||08-28-99||10:53 AM||||when the head gasket was replaced did you check the block and heads for warping? Sounds like you didn't and there is and that you've simply blown out another gasket.<p>You have to check this very carfully. If there is any warp at all then the new gasket will blow. If the warping is within a certain amount you can have the heads and or block milled to get rid of it.
Z||000004||Dale||08-28-99||12:57 PM||||Sounds like your eating some oil.  Did you do a compresion leak test?  Does it burn a bunch of oil when it sits for a little bit and then start it agian.  And was the new head gasket a retourque style.  Also check the color on the spark plugs, this will give you an idea which cylinders are the problem.  Last but not least how is Ogden these days.  I used to live north of you in Sun Valley Id.  I can relate to the snow very well!!
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||08-28-99||02:34 PM||||Patrick, HAHA<p>batboy, I am gonna do a compression test right now.<p>Orge, my father-in-law did the gaskets. He is a GM mechanic for 20 years now, but I am not sure, to be honest, if he checked for warping. I am sure he did, but to be sure, I will ask him what exactly he did.<p>Dale, (hehe that's my middle name) when I first fire the truck it spits out tiny drops of black crud. I'm not sure what it is, but I guess it would be oil. There is a big spot on the concrete where this occurs. My father-in-law said if the block is cracked, water or something could be making it run rich. He does all the labor for me for free and I don't want to bug him with my truck anymore, so I want to fix it myself.<p>I'm on my way to do a compression check. I'll post what I find. Thanx guys.....
Z||000006||theogre||08-28-99||07:59 PM||||black crud on start up.... likely a mix of soot and water out of the catalyst. Unless you are sucking coolant thru the head gasket. It's anoying but semi normal on older motors.<p>you can make sure it water by letting it hit something fresh, paper, cardboard, etc.... then you can feel for oil. 
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||08-28-99||08:56 PM||||I did a compression test... I drove the truck for about 15 minutes. Then I pulled out all the plugs. The passenger side bank were all 110 psi and the driver side bank are all 120-122 psi.
Z||000008||Dale||08-29-99||02:58 PM||||What were the color of the plugs.  Were any of them black or ecceptionaly clean.  Have you been losing any water or oil.  And is the oil still clear in the block or milky at all.
Z||000009||batboy||08-29-99||03:28 PM||||Having one bank with consistantly lower compression does not sound good. I wonder if that head is slightly warped. You may need to have that head milled and checked for cracks. If I were doing it, I'd be tempted to yank both heads off and completely rebuild them including a good valve job. Power is made or lost in the heads.
Z||000010||SCCA FIERO||08-29-99||04:38 PM||||The head with 110 reading, the plugs were black, the other head with 120-122 reading, the plugs looked fine.<p>I guess I should pull that head? If that head is to be milled, the intake needs to be milled too? Would it be better to have both heads milled the same and the intake to match both?
Z||000011||Dale||08-29-99||06:49 PM||||OK lets try this again.  Had a nice long paragraph for you but right when I was about to send it my 20 month old daughter deleted it for me ( I think she knows more about computers than I do).  Anyway, to answer your question, without a dought do both heads.  And as suggested have them rebuilt.  I am however puzzled why all the plugs on one side are black.  What kind of induction system do you have?  When you take the head off you can check for warping with a straight edge in all directions.  This does take a very good eye.  Some other things to check for are: burnt valves, excessive carbon build-up on top side of valve, scoring in cylinder walls, uneven ware in cylinders.  Its really hard without actualy looking at it.
Z||000012||batboy||08-29-99||07:11 PM||||The intake doesn't always need to be milled if the heads are milled, it depends on how much needs to be milled. Of course it's best to do the intake too. You ought to check one last thing though. If that was the head that your father-in-law had off, I'd double check the rocker arm adjustment first. Time after time I've seen people mess up the adjustment even though it's real easy to adjust hydraulic lifters.
Z||000013||Dale||08-29-99||11:27 PM||||Batboy,<br>I knew there was something I was missing.<p>Thanks
Z||000014||SCCA FIERO||08-29-99||11:34 PM||||My intake is CPI. It has a big plenium kinda like the Fiero has, but it has one injector in the top and has 6 runners.<p>My father-in-law did both gaskets. I'm not sure if he had to get them milled or if he even checked them.
Z||000015||Dale||08-30-99||07:42 PM||||Hows the truck project going?
Z||000016||SCCA FIERO||08-30-99||08:15 PM||||I still haven't figured out what it is.<p>Today I did a few things, this is what happened.... I got new Bosch plugs and installed them. I ran it for about 30 seconds and it still ran terrible (idle was rough, spitting small black liquid drops and thin black smoke). I pulled the plugs and the passenger side plugs all had a thin coat of oil. I then left those 3 plugs out and started it. It ran rough, of course, but it didn't spit black crud or smoke. What do ya think it is? I guess it's that head, but what is wrong with that head? Warped? Cracked? Knowing my luck.... both.
Z||000017||Dale||08-31-99||01:09 AM||||Well it sounds like your going to end up taking that head off.  Out of curiosity you might check the tourque on the head bolts.  The only thing that I can think of that would send oil in all cylinders is: A warped head, bad rings (not to likely), bad valve stem seals, or bad valve guides.  I am guessing the latter two of the possibilities due to the last time you blew the head gasket on that side.  I am guessing that the head probibly got hot.  If you do end up pulling the heads, check with Chevy to see if they made a hypo or turbo version of that motor and use the head bolts for that application.  They are usualy stronger and will use a higher torque setting.  Just for kicks 2000 is as high as the carbon/hydro test will go, my hypo datsun w/duel webers hit 2005 you should have seen the look on the guys face.
Z||000018||theogre||08-31-99||12:47 PM||||yeah, sounds pretty much like the head gasket let go. oil & water in 3 cyls.....<p>You'll definatly need to yank it off again. Recheck it carefully for warp. Check the block deck to. If it's ok, then definatly replace all the bolts. The ones you have are likely too stretched. I agree with getting the HO bolts if you can.
Z||000019||lowCG||08-31-99||01:02 PM||||Yeah,if that head got hot,the stem seals/guides could've gotten damaged.When you tear into it,look very carefully for signs of oil trace around the bolts,oil passage too,just too make sure.<br>Is the coolant tainted with combustion(smells like exhaust) stuff?<br>Is there any constant bubbling in the radaitor while running with the cap off?<br>Probably would be if the gasket/surfaces were to be bad.
Z||000020||SCCA FIERO||09-04-99||05:10 PM||||I am really stumped. I did a pressure test and it held 15lbs. for half an hour. I pulled the 3 plugs from the head in question, they all fired and I could smell gas after running the motor for a few seconds. Then I reinstalled those plugs and ran the truck for about a minute or so. The drivers side exhaust manifold was so hot I couldn't touch it. The passengers side was warm, but I could keep my hand on it. I think there is fuel and I know there is spark. Could there be something wrong with a sensor? I have a "Check engine" light and a "Check gauges" light. The "Check engine" light hasn't come on (I don't think) but the "Check gauges" light has. My truck needs a scan tool to retreive codes, but I don't know if there  are codes to go with the "Check guages" light.<p>I did get different readings when I did the compression test, could that be because the passengers side head wasn't as warm as the other? The drivers side were all 120-122 and the passengers side were all 110. Should the readings all be higher? I didn't push the gas pedal down when I did this test (I forgot about that). 
Z||000021||Dale||09-04-99||09:02 PM||||I was wondering how it was going.  I was lookig back through the thread and I didn't  see if you mentioned loosing water again,are you?  If the passenger side pipes are hot I would still bet on it being the head gasket, if you are getting water in that side it will create a leaner burn as well as an incomplete burn so you will smell fuel.  A leaner burn runs HOT.  An incomplete burn will result in a water and soot spot on your garage floor as Ogre stated earlier.  It sounds stupid but have someone start the truck while you take a wif of the exhaust (after it has started I cup my hand and wave a little bit towards my face)see if it smells like oil or gas.  I can tell you dont want to take your head off and dont blame you.  Who does?  Its not that hard.  Check your water level!!!
Z||000022||theogre||09-05-99||09:25 AM||||are you sure the intake is ok? If it's sucked a gasket, or cracked you could also have problems that are really odd like this.
Z||000023||Dale||09-05-99||11:01 AM||||Good point Ogre.  you have to take the manifold off before you take the head off anyway so you might as well check it first.
Z||000024||SCCA FIERO||09-05-99||04:18 PM||||Well, I started to remove the intake to get to the head. My intake is in 2 parts, an upper and lower. I removed the upper part and there was <b>OIL</b> standing in the bottom part and the whole thing is coated oil. What is causing this? Bad ring(s)? Funny thing is that most of the oil is on the opposite side of the head with fouling plugs.<p>BTW, my intake is CMFI - Central Multi-port Fuel Injection. It has one injector with 6 poppet valves and 6 huge air tunnels.
Z||000025||Dale||09-05-99||05:32 PM||||KEEP GOING!!
Z||000026||SCCA FIERO||09-06-99||04:04 PM||||I found a bolt in the passengers side head. It does not appear to be banged up, and none of the bolts to the head are missing. <p>I checked the bolts that are reacheable, without removing the rocker arms, to see if they are torqued. They were torqued correctly.<p>I haven't really found anything wrong besides to homeless bolt. I guess I will keep digging down until I find something wrong or the heads are off.
